{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PrueHRUJvdboxYuqvGYVoc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"Doc navigability & staleness check: keep the hand-written doc set honest.\n3\t\n4\tThe harness already gates the freshness of *generated* blocks (the code map —\n5\t`gen_arch_map.py --check`), but nothing checks that the **hand-written** docs\n6\tstay navigable: that every intra-repo link resolves, every doc is reachable,\n7\tand a doc hasn't been frozen beside churning code. This operationalizes the\n8\t\"verify no broken intra-doc links\" step several gates ask a human to do by hand,\n9\textending the \"the map must stay honest\" guarantee from code to docs\n10\t(process.md §3 \"Reviewability\").\n11\t\n12\tStdlib only, like trace.py / check_flows.py:\n13\t\n14\t    python scripts/check_docs.py [--root .] [--docs docs] [--entry PATH ...]\n15\t                                 [--ignore GLOB ...] [--strict-orphans] [--stale]\n16\t\n17\tIt scans root-level `*.md` plus everything under `docs/`, then reports five\n18\tfinding classes:\n19\t\n20\t  - **broken intra-repo links** (target file/dir or `#anchor` missing) — a hard\n21\t    finding; exit 1.\n22\t  - **orphan docs** (no path from an entry root: root-level `*.md`, an optional\n23\t    `docs/index.md` Map-of-Content, or a `--entry`) — a warning by default; exit\n24\t    1 only with `--strict-orphans`. A fresh scaffold legitimately has standalone\n25\t    docs (interfaces, stakeholder-needs) until the project links them, so the\n26\t    floor is warn, not fail. Docs under `docs/archive/` (frozen history) are\n27\t    exempt — orphanhood is noise there, though their links are still validated.\n28\t    A repo may also declare *expected* live-orphan classes in\n29\t    `docs/orphans-allow` (one glob per line): retained evidence — reviews,\n30\t    closed-WI specs, dual-plan round artifacts — that stays on disk but is not a\n31\t    navigation node stops warning individually (an aggregate `note` replaces the\n32\t    per-doc noise) and never fails, even under `--strict-orphans`. Only a\n33\t    *newly introduced* orphan outside every declared class still warns (or fails\n34\t    under `--strict-orphans`) — the no-new-orphan ratchet, baselining history\n35\t    without freezing it. Absent file => today's behavior unchanged, so the kit\n36\t    never surprises an existing repo.\n37\t  - **the vision tag** (process.md §4 G1's mechanizable half): the root README\n38\t    must carry the singleton `PROJECT-VISION:` tag exactly once — zero (the\n39\t    canonical vision statement is missing) or several (a re-authored variant;\n40\t    other docs point at the tag, never restate it) is a hard finding; exit 1.\n41\t    No root README at all degrades to a warning (a louder, different problem).\n42\t  - **README need coverage** (**opt-out**; process.md §4 G1): traceability is the\n43\t    kit's core value, so the root README is held to it too — every SN id cited\n44\t    anywhere in it must exist in the stakeholder-needs registry, and every\n45\t    Must/Should need in that registry must be cited somewhere in the README, so a\n46\t    requirements change mechanically ages the README. Draft/unratified needs (under\n47\t    a \"draft\" heading — SN maturity is section-as-state, §4a) are exempt from that\n48\t    floor until ratified. No delimiter markers: any\n49\t    `SN-###` in the README counts as a citation. A README opts out with an\n50\t    `<!-- sn-inventory: off -->` comment; a repo with no real needs yet (only the\n51\t    `-000` placeholder) is vacuously clean, so a fresh scaffold passes.\n52\t  - **staleness** (`--stale`, git-gated): a doc linking a *non-doc* file\n53\t    (source/asset) that was committed more recently than the doc itself — a\n54\t    \"lying map\" heuristic. Printed as a **`hint`**, a severity below `WARN`: it\n55\t    is a low-confidence nudge to look, not a finding (a linked file often\n56\t    changes without invalidating the prose), so it never counts toward the exit\n57\t    status. Degrades to a clean skip when git is unavailable or off a work tree.\n58\t    Docs under `docs/archive/` (frozen history) are exempt — staleness is noise\n59\t    there.\n60\t\n61\t  - **status-surface structure** (**warn-only**, never the exit code;\n62\t    process-options.md \"Trajectory / work-items layer\"): `docs/status.md` is the\n63\t    lean status snapshot, so (S-1) it warns past a line budget — default 120,\n64\t    `docs/status-lint` overrides with an integer or the one word `off` (which\n65\t    disables S-1..S-3); (S-2) its Open-items marker must precede the `## Scope`\n66\t    heading (open items surface at the top); (S-3) with `docs/open-items.md`\n67\t    present, every `OI-N` inside the Needs-<human> block has a `## OI-N`\n68\t    section there (no undocumented owner ask) and every section id appears in\n69\t    status.md (no orphan brief). S-3 **retires under a generated snapshot**\n70\t    (WI-202): once status.md carries the `<!-- BEGIN GENERATED STATUS -->` block,\n71\t    its open-items list is PROJECTED from open-items.md, so the two cannot\n72\t    disagree — the `status-map` freshness gate is the invariant and S-3 stands\n73\t    down (S-1/S-2 still guard the hand-authored region). S-3 extraction is\n74\t    best-effort over the template's shape; a custom layout misses warnings, never\n75\t    false-fails. Vacuous when status.md (or, for S-3, open-items.md) is absent.\n76\t\n77\tThe root `OWNER_SCRATCHPAD.md` is exempt from all of the above: it holds the\n78\thuman owner's free-form private notes and is dropped from doc discovery entirely\n79\t(agents must not read or act on it; its own header says so).\n80\t\n81\tScope (the high-value 80%): inline links `[text](dest)` and same-file/`file#frag`\n82\tanchors against GitHub-style heading slugs (plus `{#custom-id}` suffixes and\n83\t`<a name=...>`/`id=...`). Out of scope by design: reference-style links\n84\t(`[t][ref]`), images (`![alt](src)` — skipped, not existence-checked), and links\n85\tinside fenced/inline code (stripped before parsing). Anchors are only validated\n86\tagainst Markdown targets the script can parse.\n87\t\n88\tContracts: IF-002, IF-030 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n89\t\"\"\"\n90\t\n91\timport argparse\n92\timport fnmatch\n93\timport os\n94\timport re\n95\timport shutil\n96\timport subprocess\n97\timport sys\n98\tfrom pathlib import Path\n99\tfrom urllib.parse import unquote\n100\t\n101\t\n102\tdef _utf8_console():\n103\t    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n104\t    non-ASCII doc path or link target in a finding can't raise\n105\t    UnicodeEncodeError on a legacy Windows cp1252 console. Python 3.7+ streams\n106\t    expose `.reconfigure`; guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n107\t    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n108\t        try:\n109\t            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n110\t        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n111\t            pass\n112\t\n113\t\n114\t# A fenced code block opens/closes on a line of >=3 backticks or tildes.\n115\tFENCE_RE = re.compile(r\"^\\s*(`{3,}|~{3,})\")\n116\t# Inline code span: a run of N backticks closed by a run of exactly N (Common
